      "editorial": {
        "en": {
          "why_it_matters": "Takaiwa is interesting precisely because he refuses to keep jazz vocals, hip-hop, rock, electronic music and showmanship in separate boxes. The Yokohama date returns that vocabulary to a large-ensemble setting: his first solo album was built with a full big band, and this first Billboard Live tour is explicitly introducing a new Ryo Takaiwa Big Band. That makes the arranging scale and groove part of the reason to listen, not just decoration behind a singer. The current listing stops short of naming the band, so the musical promise is real but the event should not be oversold before the personnel are known.",
          "trip_verdict": "Worth keeping on the Kanto board if November 3 is open, especially for a night centered on Japanese jazz vocals, big-band arranging and genre collision. I would attend if it fits cleanly, while keeping a rarer master, one-off lineup or premiere ahead of it until the full band is announced.",
